<h1>User Onboarding Guide</h1>

<p>This guide outlines the general process for onboarding new users into the company network. The process may vary depending on the department, but there are key steps that should always be followed.</p>

<hr>

<h2>External users</h2>

<p>When onboarding an external user, the following steps must be completed:</p>

<p>- The user must physically sign an IT security document, confirming acceptance of company policies</p>
<p>- The user should log in to their PC for the first time within the IT department</p>
<p>- After the initial login, accessing the system off-premises becomes easier</p>
<p>- Ensure the user is assigned all relevant Active Directory groups</p>
<p>- Provide VPN access (Palo Alto) if required</p>

<hr>

<h2>Internal users</h2>

<p>When onboarding an internal user, the following should be verified:</p>

<p>- The user has the correct Active Directory group memberships</p>
<p>- The user can successfully log in via Active Directory</p>

<p>Access considerations:</p>

<p>- If the user only works on-site, VPN access is not required</p>
<p>- If the user works off-premises, VPN access must be configured</p>

<p>Hardware considerations:</p>

<p>- If the user is assigned a laptop, they must sign a document for asset tracking</p>

<hr>

<h2>Customer service onboarding</h2>

<p>For users joining the customer service department, additional steps are required:</p>

<p>- Ensure the user has a 3CX login</p>
<p>- Ensure the user has a 2FA token</p>
<p>- Ensure the user has access to Inexso for email handling</p>
<p>- Provide a working headset</p>
<p>- Laptop access is optional and requires approval from the department manager</p>

<p>If the user has special hardware requirements (e.g., mouse or keyboard), the request must go through their manager, who will then coordinate with IT for ordering.</p>

<hr>

<h2>Notes</h2>

<p>This is a general onboarding guide. Processes may vary depending on the department, and additional details will be added over time.</p>
